integrated_rules:
Treat the claim “alternative energies effectively replace fossil fuels” as true only if the proponent shows **(i) rapid-in-practice decarbonization speed** and **(ii) operational feasibility at scale**—and treat it as false (with **natural gas as a necessary bridge**) if the proponent’s evidence shows that a true replacement cannot eliminate fossil-fuel use fast enough on a realistic net-zero/climate timeline.

Concretely, conclude **effective replacement** when all of the following are evidenced:
1) **Capability/scale know-how exists**: there are already the knowledge and tools to carry out the transition at scale (not merely a theoretical possibility);
2) **Operational precedent for high fossil-free electricity** exists, such as real countries running on very high renewable shares (e.g., Iceland ~85%, Paraguay ~99.9%, Costa Rica ~99.78%, Norway ~98%), indicating the transition is not purely hypothetical;
3) **Timeline constraint is met**: the plan can rapidly deliver the needed energy services while cutting emissions fast enough that a large fraction of required reductions are achieved immediately—meaning that building and integrating alternatives (generation + transmission + storage + and crucially end-use conversion such as electrification of fossil-dependent uses) can occur quickly enough for “true replacement” rather than leaving fossil fuel use in place for too long;
4) If the plan relies on **nuclear as the quickest, low-land, low-carbon complement**, the argument also plausibly preserves carbon-free gains despite nuclear accident risks (e.g., avoiding a Fukushima-like scenario that would negate carbon-free electricity).

Otherwise, conclude **alternative energies are not an effective substitute for fossil fuels** for the purposes of climate timelines, and that **natural gas is a necessary bridge** because existing fossil-based assets enable rapid emissions reductions via relatively fast “fuel switching,” while alternatives (and grid + end-use conversion) cannot scale/eliminate fossil use quickly enough. As supporting context for this “cannot scale fast enough” determination, note that if in large-country settings (e.g., the U.S.) alternative-energy use has historically **never exceeded ~20%**, that is evidence the speed/capacity problem is real unless the proponent supplies credible, plan-specific reasons it will be overcome.
